  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nAutumn has finally arrived to South Texas. At the Corpus ASOS this \nmorning, we hit 54F. While normal low temperatures don't drop to \nthis value until late-November, the first occurrence of sub-55F low \ntemperatures (according to the period of record) falls on October \n19th, so we aren't too far off for our first cooldown. This drier \nand cooler airmass will stick around through tomorrow, where Sunday \nmorning's low temperatures will fall to the upper 40s across the \nBrush Country and inland coastal plains, while along the coast, lows \nwill generally drop to the 50s (low 60s on the barrier islands). \n\nThe front that moved through this weekend has stalled offshore. In \nthe mid-levels a weak low on the tail of this front will slowly \ndrift westward as the mid-level high pressure slides into the \nSoutheastern US. This weak mid-level low will turn surface winds to \nbe more easterly while also re-introducing 1.5-1.7\" PWAT values \ntowards coastal zones (per the GEFS). The combined mid- level \nenergy and increasing PWATs will bring back showers across the \nwestern Gulf Monday, but how close this makes it to the Coastal \nBend is low in confidence. For the same time frame, the ECMWF \nEnsemble has 1.3-1.6\" PWATs meaning less opportunity for any \noffshore showers to push to far inland before evaporating out. As \nsuch, PoP values for Monday generally remain under 30% and are \nmore concentrated for coastal zones south of Port Aransas. \n\nCoastal rain chances continue Tuesday (again, low in confidence) as \nthe mid-level feature hangs around the western Gulf waters, but all \nguidance shows this low filling in by Wednesday and mid-levels \ndrying out starting mid-week.\n\nMarine hazards will slowly diminish through the next 24 hr periods \nwith bays recovering first this afternoon followed by the open \nwaters overnight. Coastal flooding is low in confidence given the \nshort duration for long-swell period to develop. The rip current \nrisk drops to moderate starting tonight. \n\nTemperatures will continue to climb back to normal and then above-\nnormal values by the next weekend.",
